Copyright: 2013-- scikit-bio development team
Comment: alr() and ilr(), with their default (Gram-Schmidt / Helmert) basis,
 the conversion of a sequential binary partition and the basis checks, follow
 alr(), ilr(), _gram_schmidt_basis(), sbp_basis() and _check_basis() from
 scikit-bio 0.7.4 (skbio/stats/composition/_base.py). balance() is one
 coordinate of ilr() with a sequential binary partition, through the same
 sbp_basis(). vlr() follows vlr() and pairwise_vlr() from the same file,
 computing each variance from differences of centred logs rather than from a
 covariance matrix. ancom() follows ancom() and _log_compare() from
 skbio/stats/composition/_ancom.py, with the per-feature p-value adjustment
 of _adjust_pvalues() and the grouping checks of _check_grouping() from
 _utils.py; its tests compute each log ratio's statistic directly, in C.
 dirmult_ttest() follows dirmult_ttest(), _dirmult_draw() and
 _welch_draw_stats() from skbio/stats/composition/_dirmult.py, with the
 contrast rules of _check_trt_ref_groups() from _utils.py; its Gamma draws
 come from ecodive's own sampler (src/rng_dist.c), not NumPy's.
 The rest of these files, including log_ratio(), rho_p() and phi_s(), is
 Copyright 2026 ecodive authors, under the MIT license above.
