Files: src/ancombc.c R/ancombc.r R/ancombc_posthoc.r
Copyright: 2013-- scikit-bio development team; Huang Lin
Comment: ancombc() and ancombc2() are a C/R port of ancombc(), ancombc2() and
 _ancombc_core() with their helpers (_transform_data(), _estimate_params_dense(),
 _estimate_params_sparse(), _lstsq_sparse_batch(), _apply_pinv(),
 _solve_sparse_iter(), _r_fit_info(), _r_rebase_categorical(),
 _calc_grouped_var_cov(), _estimate_bias_em(), _init_bias_params(),
 _estimate_bias_var(), _sample_fractions(), _adjust_variance(),
 _calc_statistics(), _calc_pvalues()) from scikit-bio 0.7.4
 (skbio/stats/composition/_ancombc.py); ancombc_global(), ancombc_pairwise(),
 ancombc_dunnett(), ancombc_trend() and structural_zeros() port the
 ANCOMBCResult methods global_test(), pairwise_test(), dunnett_test() and
 trend_test() (_global_stats(), _pairwise_test(), _mdfdr_pairwise(),
 _dunnett_test(), _mdfdr_dunnett(), _dunn_global(), _trend_test(),
 _constrain_est(), _constrain_est_identity(), _safe_inverse_spd(),
 _l_infty()) and struc_zero() from the same file. All are released by
 scikit-bio under the BSD-3
 license below. scikit-bio's implementation "is based on an analysis of the
 source code of the R package ANCOMBC" (Bioconductor, by Huang Lin), which is
 under the Artistic License 2.0, so these files carry both notices. ANCOMBC's
 source (2.8.1 and 2.14.0) was also read, to check the port.
 How these files differ from the originals (Artistic License 2.0, section 4):
 they are a modified version, restructured for ecodive. The per-feature fits,
 variances, the bias EM and the sampling fractions are threaded C over blocks
 of features (src/ancombc.c), with ecodive's own singular value decompositions
 (one-sided Jacobi) of each feature's masked design, and SciPy's Nelder-Mead
 rules (see the SciPy stanza) for the EM's minimisations as scikit-bio uses;
 the design is built with R's model.matrix() instead of patsy (covariates named
 and ordered as in R); the post-hoc bootstraps draw from ecodive's PCG
 samplers (src/rng_dist.c), one stream per feature; the trend test's
 constrained estimates come from a primal active-set QP solver written for
 ecodive (qp_solve() in src/ancombc.c) in place of SLSQP or quadprog;
 comparisons are labelled with level names; the result is ecodive's
 per-feature data frame; and
 random effects, scikit-bio's aggregator and ANCOMBC's preprocessing,
 structural zeros and sensitivity analysis are not included. The user-visible
 differences are listed in the documentation (?ancombc, "Differences from
 scikit-bio and ANCOMBC"). ecodive is a separate package, which doesn't stop
 ANCOMBC from being installed.
 The rest of these files is Copyright 2026 ecodive authors, under the MIT
 license above.
