Copyright: Jeffrey T. Leek, W. Evan Johnson, Hilary S. Parker, Elana J.
 Fertig, Andrew E. Jaffe, Yuqing Zhang, John D. Storey and Leonardo Collado
 Torres
Comment: batch_combat() in src/batch_combat.c and R/batch_combat.r is a C
 and R port of ComBat() and its helpers aprior(), bprior(), postmean(),
 postvar(), it.sol() and int.eprior() from sva 3.60.0 (Bioconductor 3.23;
 R/ComBat.R and R/helper.R), by the authors named in sva's DESCRIPTION
 (which has no other copyright line), under the Artistic License 2.0.
 How these files differ from the originals (Artistic License 2.0, section 4):
 they are a modified version, restructured for ecodive. The fits,
 standardisation, priors, shrinkage and adjustment are threaded C over a
 table read in any of ecodive's formats (src/batch_combat.c), with one
 pivoted QR of the design (src/feature_lm.c) instead of the normal
 equations; the non-parametric priors weigh the other features by their
 likelihoods on the log scale instead of multiplying densities that can
 underflow; the shrinkage iteration's tolerance and an iteration cap are
 arguments (`tol`, `max_iters`), and NaN stops it with an error; `counts`
 is samples x features by default (`margin`) and can be transformed first
 (`norm`); covariates come from a `formula` over `data` instead of a model
 matrix `mod`, and `batch` and `data` follow ecodive's per-sample rules;
 missing values are refused; a batch of one sample turns on `mean_only`
 with a warning instead of a message, and features constant within a
 batch are left unchanged without a message; and the prior plots are
 replaced by plot.ecodive_batch(). The user-visible differences are listed
 in the documentation (?batch_combat, "Differences from sva"). ecodive is a
 separate package, which doesn't stop sva from being installed.
 data-raw/expected/batch_combat.r (not in the built package) calls sva to
 record reference outputs. The rest of these files is Copyright 2026
